WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:04.960
Troopers' Memorial is an important war
memorial of the colonial New Zealand period.

00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:10.600
Opened in 1908 it was created as a lasting reminder of the contribution of Southland's war dead

00:00:11.380 --> 00:00:18.900
from the Boer War in 1900 to 1902.
The memorial was the first for the relatively new city of Invercargill.

00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:22.540
While only 24 names are present on the Troopers' Memorial,

00:00:22.820 --> 00:00:26.360
they represent those killed during or from the Boer War.

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:33.160
Southland sent a contingent of 273 men of its population of roughly 22,000.

00:00:33.340 --> 00:00:38.080
This contingent, the fourth contingent, would be known as the rough riders
as they were all largely civilian

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:43.280
soldiers with little to no training.
The Troopers' Memorial stands as a

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:47.680
permanent reminder of new Zealand's role
in the Boer War and particularly those

00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:50.620
Southland soldiers killed in act of service.

